4. throw vs throws + finally explanation
throw: Used inside a method to manually generate an exception.
Example: throw new IllegalArgumentException();
throws: Used in method signature to declare potential exceptions.
Example: void read() throws IOException {}
finally: Commonly used to close files, release database connections, or free resources, ensuring
execution in all circumstances.
5. Custom Checked Exception + Propagation
Example:
class MyException extends Exception { MyException(String msg){ super(msg);} }
void m1() throws MyException { throw new MyException('Error'); }
void m2() throws MyException { m1(); }
void m3(){
try{ m2(); }
catch(MyException e){ System.out.println('Handled: ' + e.getMessage()); }
}
Here, the exception moves from m1 ﬁ m2 ﬁ m3 until handled.
